#====================================================================
# E999 draft - aarch64 char is unsigned: a -1 sentinel becomes index 255
# Proposed document: drafts/E999-draft-aarch64-unsigned-char-negative-index.md
#
# 格式规则:
#   - key: value（冒号后一个空格，value 到行尾）
#   - 多行值: 下一行以空格开头 = 续行
#   - 列表值: 逗号分隔
#   - 正则值: | 分隔多个模式
#   - 以 # 开头的行为注释
#====================================================================

id:                     E3524
trigger_tool:           clang|gcc|make
phase:                  runtime
error_signature:        Signal 11 \(core dumped\)|SIGSEGV|index out of bounds|wrong nucleotides|garbage characters in translated output|all codons map to the same residue

severity:               3
category:               platform, char-signedness, out-of-bounds, wrong-results
match_keywords:         unsigned char, char signedness, signed char, -1, 255, array index, out of bounds, aarch64, arm64, direction, reverse strand, SIGSEGV
title:                  Do not return -1 through a char: on aarch64 it becomes 255 and any arithmetic or array index built from it goes out of bounds

root_cause:             aarch64 has unsigned plain char by default (x86 is signed). A helper declared as "char f() { return cond ? 1 : -1; }" returns 255 on aarch64, and the value is then promoted to int 255 wherever it is used. When that value is an array index or an index multiplier, every access lands far outside the buffer: reads return garbage (silently wrong results) and writes or larger buffers produce SIGSEGV. The compiler does not flag this because the narrowing happens in the return statement, not in a comparison.
affected_scope:         Any legacy C/C++ code that encodes a tri-state or a direction/sign flag in a char. Typical victims are strand/frame direction helpers, "not found" sentinels, and table lookups keyed by a small signed value. The observable result is a silently wrong answer on small inputs and a crash on large ones, so it is easily mistaken for a data problem.
verification_cmd:       grep -rn --include=*.c --include=*.cpp --include=*.h --include=*.hpp -E 'char[[:space:]]+[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*[[:space:]]*\([^)]*\)[^;{]*\{[^}]*-[[:space:]]*1' . ; echo 'static char f(){return -1;} int main(){return (int)f()==-1?0:1;}' > /tmp/p.c && cc /tmp/p.c -o /tmp/p && /tmp/p ; echo "exit=$? (0 on x86, 1 on aarch64)"

rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           widen-the-return-type
rec_description:        Return the sign/direction value as int (and hold it in an int local). The value is used as an arithmetic operand and as an index offset, so int is its natural type; char was only ever a size optimisation.
rec_command:            sed -i -E 's/^([[:space:]]*)char([[:space:]]+getDirection[[:space:]]*\\([^)]*\\))/\\1int\\2/' <file>   # then widen the matching local variable too
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               source-patch

fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      clamp-before-indexing
fallback_description:   When the return type is ABI-frozen, never use the value directly: sign-extend it explicitly (int d = (signed char) raw;) before any arithmetic or indexing, or clamp it into the valid range and assert on out-of-range.
fallback_command:       Replace "offset + direction*(i+k)" with "offset + i + k" for the forward case and an explicit reversed index for the backward case.
fallback_auto_fix:      false
fallback_impl:          source-patch

forbid_1_pattern:       .*-fsigned-char.*|.*-funsigned-char.*|.*\(int\)[[:space:]]*\(unsigned char\).*index|.*disable.*bounds.*check
forbid_1_reason:        A global -fsigned-char/-funsigned-char flips every char in the translation unit and can break unrelated byte handling; masking the symptom by clamping the index hides that the value is simply the wrong type, and the reverse-strand results stay wrong rather than failing.
forbid_1_consequence:   The wrong-answer half of the bug survives (small inputs still produce silently incorrect output), which is worse than the crash because nothing signals it.

principles:             P005, P013
doc_file:               drafts/E999-draft-aarch64-unsigned-char-negative-index.md
last_updated:           2026-10-15
source_event:           plast 2.3.2 on HarmonyOS aarch64: reading frames 4/5/6 translated to constant K, testCompositeDatabase2 died with SIGSEGV; root cause ReadingFrameSequenceIterator.hpp getDirection()

